# Per-deployment settings for yamale-custodian.service.
# Install at /etc/yamale/custodian.env.
#
# CUSTODIAN_ROLE is the one setting that must differ between the two
# deployments, and everything else follows from it. custodian on one host,
# recovery on the other — never both on one, and never `device`, which the
# service refuses because the device's share belongs on the device.
#
# The sealing key and the pepper MUST NOT live inside CUSTODIAN_DIR. The service
# refuses to start if either does, because "back up the store directory" is the
# one instruction an operator will certainly follow, and a key inside it makes
# that backup a plaintext copy of every share.
#
# Write both secrets as hex on this host: `openssl rand -hex 32 > FILE`, mode
# 0600, readable by the service user. Exactly 32 raw bytes is also accepted,
# untouched; anything else is refused with that command in the message.
#
# CUSTODIAN_NOTIFY_COMMAND names the program that reaches account holders.
# Recovery refuses to start without one, which is the correct behaviour for a
# deployment that cannot actually reach anybody: a notifier that succeeds
# without notifying is worse than none, because it makes every other rule in the
# recovery process run silently.
#
# tools/notify is a reference implementation and writes each notice to a spool
# directory as a file. That is deliberately dull: the contract the custodian
# depends on is that a NON-ZERO EXIT ABORTS THE RECOVERY, and a local file write
# either succeeds or fails immediately with no network in the way. Move the
# spool with whatever already delivers mail here.
#
# YAMALE_NOTIFY_DIR reaches the notify program because the service passes on
# exactly one family of variables, YAMALE_NOTIFY_*, and nothing else. A variable
# under any other name will NOT be visible to it — the first version of this
# passed no environment at all, and the reference program could not be
# configured by any means.

CUSTODIAN_ROLE=custodian
CUSTODIAN_DIR=/var/lib/yamale/custodian
CUSTODIAN_SEALING_KEY=/etc/yamale/custodian.key
CUSTODIAN_PEPPER=/etc/yamale/custodian.pepper
CUSTODIAN_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:8099
CUSTODIAN_NOTIFY_COMMAND=/opt/yamale/bin/yamale-notify
YAMALE_NOTIFY_DIR=/var/spool/yamale-notices

# Gone with the threshold protocol: CUSTODIAN_POOL (there are no Paillier
# pre-parameters to keep ready) and CUSTODIAN_SECOND_FACTOR_ABOVE (nothing
# co-signs, so no service is present at a payment to demand anything). The
# second factor moved to RECOVERY, where it is not a threshold but a ladder:
# an account is asked for the strongest thing it has enrolled.
#
# Leaving either in the unit is not harmless — the service refuses an unknown
# flag and does not start.
